  "response": "I read the fixture source alongside the scan output to ground the diagnosis (`src/billing.ts`, `src/date.ts`, `src/billing.test.ts`). I have **not** edited anything — that fixture is scanner calibration input, so changing it would move the eval baseline.\n\n## Ranked risk factors from the scan\n\n| # | Finding | Why it matters for a date-boundary flake |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `crime_00012` — Temporal Recklessness, `src/billing.ts` L57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273 | Five separate `Date.now()` reads inside one function; L92/L273 feed tier comparisons |\n| 2 | `crime_00014` — God Function, `generateInvoice` L50–253 | 204 lines, no seams — the clock reads are scattered where no test can reach them |\n| 3 | `crime_00033` / `crime_00032` — Contract Split-Brain on `Invoice` / `User` | `createdAt` exists in `src/billing.ts` but is **absent** from the `src/api/state.ts` declarations |\n| 4 | `crime_00025` / `crime_00031` — Test That Proves Nothing | `billing.test.ts` has one test with zero assertions and one `toBeTruthy()` |\n\n## Most likely cause: the `daysSince` loyalty tier at `billing.ts:92`\n\n```ts\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\nif (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.1);\nelse if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);\nelse if (daysSince > 90)  discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);\n```\n\nThree defects compound into exactly the symptom described:\n\n1. **Live clock vs. fixed fixture.** Any test that pins `user.createdAt` to an absolute date and asserts a discount tier has an answer that changes as wall time advances. `Math.floor` makes the flip instantaneous — the tier changes at one specific millisecond, so the suite passes for weeks and then starts failing at a midnight it was never told about.\n2. **Fixed-millisecond day math.** `1000 * 60 * 60 * 24` is the same defect the scanner charges separately as `crime_00017` (DST-Naive Day Math) in `src/date.ts:52`. A fixture built as \"90 calendar days ago\" in a DST-observing zone is 90 days ±1 hour in milliseconds — a one-hour window on either side of a transition where `daysSince` computes one lower than intended and the tier silently drops.\n3. **Strict `>` on round boundaries.** The thresholds are 90/180/365, and a fixture written as \"exactly 90 days old\" lands on `daysSince === 90`, which fails `> 90`. Combined with (2), whether a boundary fixture reads 89, 90, or 91 depends on elapsed time and host timezone.\n\nTwo aggravating factors the scan flags separately:\n\n- **Non-atomic clock reads.** `createdAt` (L57) and the `daysSince` computation (L92) are *different* `Date.now()` calls. A test asserting on both `invoice.createdAt` and the discount can see them straddle a boundary. Same duplicated formula appears again in `recommendUpgrade` (L273), so fixing one leaves the other drifting.\n- **`crime_00033`.** If your test fixture is typed against `src/api/state.ts`'s `Invoice`/`User` (2 fields, no `createdAt`) rather than the billing-local ones, `Date.now() - undefined` is `NaN`, `Math.floor(NaN)` is `NaN`, every comparison is false, and the discount silently becomes 0 with no error. That failure is deterministic rather than flaky, so check it if the test fails *consistently* on one machine.\n\nThe existing tests can't distinguish any of this — `crime_00025`/`crime_00031` confirm nothing in `billing.test.ts` asserts on billing behaviour at all.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\n**Step 1 — inject a clock and read it once** (matches the scanner's `inject_clock` suggested action):\n\n```ts\nexport type Clock = () => number;\nconst systemClock: Clock = () => Date.now();\n\nexport function generateInvoice(\n  user: User,\n  items: InvoiceLine[],\n  paymentMethod?: string,\n  now: Clock = systemClock,\n): Invoice {\n  const at = now();                    // one read, used everywhere below\n  const createdAt = at;\n  const metadata = { /* … */ runAt: new Date(at).toISOString() };\n  const daysSince = tenureDays(user.createdAt, at);\n  // …\n}\n```\n\nOne `at` for the whole invoice removes the straddle between L57 and L92, and makes the invoice internally consistent by construction.\n\n**Step 2 — extract the tier decision as a pure, calendar-aware helper** used by both `generateInvoice` and `recommendUpgrade`, killing the duplication:\n\n```ts\nconst DAY_MS = 86_400_000;\n\n/** Whole days elapsed, floored. Boundary is inclusive: 90 days old qualifies for the 90-day tier. */\nexport function tenureDays(createdAt: number, at: number): number {\n  if (!Number.isFinite(createdAt)) throw new TypeError(\"user.createdAt is required\");\n  return Math.floor((at - createdAt) / DAY_MS);\n}\n\nexport function loyaltyRate(plan: User[\"plan\"], days: number): number {\n  if (plan === \"free\") return 0;\n  if (days >= 365) return 0.1;\n  if (days >= 180) return 0.05;\n  if (days >= 90) return 0.02;\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nNote the `>` → `>=` change: pick a boundary convention and write it down. Which direction is a product decision, but leaving it implicit is what makes \"exactly 90 days\" a coin flip. The `Number.isFinite` guard turns the `crime_00033` `NaN` path into a loud failure instead of a silent zero.\n\nIf tenure is meant to be *calendar* days rather than 86.4M-ms units, `DAY_MS` arithmetic is wrong at every DST transition and this should go through `Temporal.PlainDate.since()` or the equivalent in Luxon / date-fns-tz — the scanner's `use_calendar_aware_lib` action on `crime_00017`.\n\n**Step 3 — pin the clock in tests**, which is now possible without monkey-patching:\n\n```ts\nconst DAY = 86_400_000;\nconst T0 = Date.parse(\"2026-06-15T12:00:00Z\");\n\nit.each([[89, 0], [90, 0.02], [179, 0.02], [180, 0.05], [364, 0.05], [365, 0.1]])(\n  \"tenure %i days → rate %f\",\n  (days, rate) => expect(loyaltyRate(\"pro\", days)).toBe(rate),\n);\n\nit(\"uses one clock instant for createdAt and tenure\", () => {\n  const inv = generateInvoice(userAgedDays(90, T0), items, undefined, () => T0);\n  expect(inv.createdAt).toBe(T0);\n  expect(inv.discountCents).toBe(Math.round(inv.subtotalCents * 0.02));\n});\n```\n\nThat covers each boundary from both sides at a fixed instant, so the tests stop depending on the day they run or the timezone of the runner.\n\n**Out of scope but adjacent:** if the failing assertion is on a *date string* rather than a discount amount, the cause is instead `crime_00009` — `dateLabel()` at `src/date.ts:41` mixes `getUTCFullYear()` with local `getMonth()`/`getDate()`, which disagree by the host's UTC offset for part of every day, and also emits a zero-indexed month. `src/billing.ts` doesn't import `src/date.ts`, so I'd only chase that if the failing test touches `date.ts` directly.",
